WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:21.740
THE STATE OF GOOGLE:
I’m sure you’re all familiar with Google’s

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:22.740
Moonshot factory.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.490
It’s the home of Google’s most ambitious
projects form Google Glass and Waymo to Loon

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.210
and Verily.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:36.020
Even before the moonshot factory was formally
founded in 2010, Google was at the forefront

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.980
of technological development and advancement.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.570
But, it appears that this has been changing
over the past couple of years.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:50.210
Like any corporation, Google has become more
focused on profits and pleasing shareholders

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.310
than making massive bets.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:57.680
We no longer see those over the top presentations
from Sergey Brin and Larry Page.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.520
Nor do we hear about Google making much tangible
progress with self driving.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.939
This trend has simply been exacerbated by
the recession.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:12.250
In September, Google cancelled up to half
of their R&amp;D projects across the company.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.439
And more recently, it was reported that Google
is diverting much of its moonshot factory

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:19.600
efforts into revenue generating projects.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.970
This trend doesn’t just apply to Google’s
Moonshot factory either.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.240
It’s actually a larger trend that can be
observed across the company.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.570
Many Google veterans have raised concerns
that Google’s culture has become unrecognizable.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.640
And the recent layoffs are just one example
of that.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.350
The old Google would have done anything to
prevent layoffs similar to Southwest.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:45.689
If you didn’t know, Southwest has never
done layoffs despite facing way more tumultuous

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:52.910
times than Google from the 1973 oil crisis
and 2001 to 2008 and the pandemic.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:58.530
Meanwhile, Google is posting near record profits
and chose to layoff 12,000 people mostly due

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.530
to shareholder concerns.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:04.930
But likely the best example of Google turning
the corner is the fact that they dropped their

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.610
famous slogan “Don’t Be Evil”.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:13.450
In 2015, Google added a new motto to their
code of conduct “Do the right thing” before

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.500
dropping the original slogan in 2018.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.140
This massive shift didn’t just happen over
night though.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.510
So, here’s the slow devolution of Google
from being the ambitious startup that was

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:31.040
at the forefront of tech to being the background
company that just wants to stay in their lane.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:37.280
COMPLACENT EMPLOYEES:
When you think about a career at Google, one

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.540
of the first things that come to mind are
the high compensation, prestige, and influence.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.650
For decades, Google has prided themselves
with paying the absolute highest salaries

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.790
and from a statistical standpoint, this is
still very much true.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:58.140
In 2021, Alphabet once again topped the median
employee salary list coming in with a median

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.709
compensation of $295,000.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:06.670
Combine this with the insane perks that Google
offers like their free food and remote working

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:11.650
opportunities, and you would think that Google
employees are living the high life and objectively

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.450
speaking they are.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.819
But, happiness is very much a relative thing,
and for people working at Google, all of these

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:20.870
perks are very much the norm.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:25.709
Not to mention, Google isn’t the only company
where they can enjoy these benefits.

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:31.280
Until recently, the number of options that
these people had was unprecedented leading

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.489
them to feel unsatisfied and yes undercompensated.

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:40.540
I know that’s probably hard to believe,
so I’ve got survey results to back this

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.540
up.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:47.629
Back in early 2022, Business Insider found
that only a mere 46% of surveyed Google employees

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:49.400
were happy with their compensation.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:56.660
In other words, over half of Google’s workforce
thinks that their compensation isn’t competitive.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.380
And this feeling is simply getting stronger
every year.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:05.959
Back in 2020, 64% of Google’s employees
felt that their compensation was competitive.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:10.850
This number dropped to 56% in 2021 before
dropping to 46%.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:15.409
Much of these can be explained by even stronger
offers coming from startups like Coinbase

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:16.409
and Stripe.

00:04:16.409 --> 00:04:20.400
But, it’s not just the compensation that
Googlers are upset with either.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.389
Many also feel that the company is becoming
more bureaucratic and less transparent.

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:29.990
Within the cloud division, for example, only
54% of employees feel that the promotional

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.830
process is fair.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.270
You can decipher this in two ways.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.320
Either, you can think that these guys are
simply spoiled brats who don’t realize how

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.479
good they have it.

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:43.660
Or, you can think that these truly guys truly
do have a lot more potential like become a

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.800
founder or an executive.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:51.860
Either way though, the end feeling is the
same: unhappy and unmotivated employees.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:57.850
And this feeling only leads to two things:
high attrition and/or a stagnant workforce.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.690
The high attrition is no secret.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:03.490
At Alphabet, the median tenure is only a mere
1.3 years.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:08.930
This statistic is skewed by new hires, but
that doesn’t really make the statistic all

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.610
that much better.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.800
Speaking of new hires, Google has been aggressively
recruiting and growing their workforce over

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.259
the past 10 years.

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:22.990
Between 2009 and 2021, Google’s workforce
grew from 20,000 employees to 156,000 employees.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:28.590
And realistically speaking, it’s significantly
harder to maintain the same culture and caliber

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.470
across 156,000 people than it is to maintain
it across 20,000 people.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.130
It’s more than likely that a substantial
portion of these new hires were simply looking

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.000
for the compensation and resume boost.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.740
And when you have too many of these people,
it’s just a matter of time until your company

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.620
becomes soulless.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:50.800
COMPLACENT EXECUTIVES:
It’s common for founders to have a bunch

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.930
of beef with shareholders.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.160
Mark Zuckerburg, for example, is actively
fighting the entire world when it comes to

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.550
pursuing the Metaverse.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:04.690
This was very much the same case with Google’s
founders: Larry Page &amp; Sergey Brin.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:09.360
And this strife between investors and executives
is very much what gave Google it’s unique

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.360
culture.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:16.420
In 2001, for example, Larry Page went ahead
and fired all of Google’s project managers.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:17.420
The reasoning?

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:22.190
Well, Page felt that engineers didn’t need
anyone to give them directions and that they

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.560
shouldn’t be forced to follow arbitrary
deadlines.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.849
HR did end up reversing this decision, but
this gives us a glimpse of just how Page felt

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:34.710
about managers and why Google ended up being
such an engineering driven company.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:38.919
Similarly, Sergey felt that no one should
be 200 feet away from food.

00:06:38.919 --> 00:06:44.639
And this is why Google has so much food everywhere
despite it being rather uneconmical.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:49.850
Many of Google’s most iconic moves were
such unorthodox rebellious actions.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.880
Both Page and Brin had a history of completing
acquisitions without consulting with anyone

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.560
else in the company including even the official
CEO.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.280
This was the case with even Android and Google
Earth.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.520
We should also note that this approach led
to a bunch of failures like Google Glass,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.910
but the takeaway isn’t that the founders
were rebellious.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:14.630
The takeaway is that the founders didn’t
care about bureaucracy or angry shareholders,

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:18.910
and that their main goal was simply doing
the best thing for the company.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.160
This is actually the main reason that both
founders actually left Google.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.370
They felt that they could no longer do what
was right for the company and that there was

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.790
simply too much red tape to keep fighting.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:32.780
Conversely, Google’s new leadership isn’t
put off by the red tape.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:38.340
In fact, you could even argue that they like
the red tape as it gives them purpose, definition,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.569
and meaning.

00:07:39.569 --> 00:07:44.550
Their jobs as executives is to make shareholders
happy and to constantly grow earnings quarter

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:45.960
after quarter.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.990
And from this perspective, Sundar Pichai has
hit it out of the park.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:55.009
Since he became CEO in 2015, Google stock
has grown as much as 5X.

00:07:55.009 --> 00:07:59.070
And even with the recession, Google is up
4X.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.440
A similar story can be seen with Google’s
fundamentals as well.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:08.599
Since 2015, Google’s revenue has grown from
$70 billion to $280 billion and their net

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:12.240
income has grown from $15 billion to over
$70 billion.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.090
So, clearly, Sundar has given shareholders
exactly what they wanted, but this very much

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.659
came with a cost.

00:08:19.659 --> 00:08:24.099
To accomplish such growth, Google couldn’t
focus on random out there projects that were

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:25.870
very much a gamble.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:30.250
Instead, Google had to double down on their
revenue generating sectors such as ads and

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.909
the cloud and focus on making these endeavors
as profitable as possible.

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:37.060
And that’s exactly what they did.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:38.570
They cut out all the fluff.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:44.260
But, while this seemed like fluff for shareholders,
this was what made Google Google.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:50.350
And without it, Google became just another
soulless tech corporation.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:55.130
PUBLIC SENTIMENT:
While much of the new Google has to do with

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:59.860
a shift in leadership and employee culture,
not all of it can be attributed to Google

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.050
themselves.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.950
Much of the old Google was formed by public
sentiment and much of the new Google is also

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.670
formed by public sentiment.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:12.110
In fact, you could argue that Google’s public
sentiment was what made them so successful

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.820
in the first place.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:15.610
Take Gmail for example.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.980
Much of Gmail’s appeal came from the fact
that it was exclusive, had generous storage,

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.510
and was anti Yahoo and Microsoft.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.890
The same thing could be said about Chrome.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:29.250
Much of Chrome’s appeal came from the fact
that it was fast, reliable, and anti Internet

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.450
Explorer and Microsoft.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:36.600
Google had built this image as the white knight
savior tech company that wasn’t evil.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.670
But, the sentiment around Google has shifted
substantially over the past 15 years.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:46.190
Google is no longer the startup taking on
the big players.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:47.990
Google is the big player.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:52.740
Not to mention, Google has also built up a
rather negative reputation when it comes to

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.680
data collection and privacy.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:10:00.010
We’ve all seen those memes that Alexa, Google,
and Siri are always listening.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.640
This sentiment hurts the company in two ways.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.770
For one, the public is less likely to support
new products that come from the company.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:11.820
A great example of this is with Google Fi,
their cell phone plan.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:16.130
Objectively speaking, Google Fi offers extremely
generous plans that come in way cheaper than

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:17.540
the competition.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:22.600
This is the same formula that Google used
with Gmail and Google Fiber to great success.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.920
But, most people don’t even know about Google
Fi because the cellular space already had

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.810
an anti carrier player: T-Mobile.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:37.260
If anything, Google was viewed more like AT&amp;T
or Verizon than they were as T-Mobile.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.930
And this makes sense because Google was essentially
trying to control you smartphone, your OS,

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.390
the things that you do on your phone, and
your connection.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:50.200
And if the public isn’t willing to support
a cell phone plan, it’s much less likely

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.860
that they’ll support something that’s
truly out there.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.399
I mean just take a look at Facebook.

00:10:55.399 --> 00:11:01.000
Sure, the Metaverse is underwhelming, but
I’d bet that much of its criticism comes

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.279
from people’s already negative opinion of
Facebook.

00:11:04.279 --> 00:11:08.889
The Metaverse wouldn’t be nearly as ridiculed
if it wasn’t associated with Facebook and

00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:10.100
Mark Zuckerberg.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:15.320
For obvious reasons, Google doesn’t want
to go down the same path, so they’ve decided

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.640
to simply play it on the safe side.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.029
Aside from the public not be receptive to
new products, a negative stigma also makes

00:11:22.029 --> 00:11:24.850
it much more difficult to attract the best
talent.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.690
Google is not the top company that engineering
students want to work for.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:34.320
The top companies that engineering students
want to work for are SpaceX and Tesla, and

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.850
those companies don’t even pay crazy salaries
or offer ridiculous perks.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.600
As more and more talented engineers flock
to such companies and startups, it’s no

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.730
wonder why Google is no longer pushing the
boundaries.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:53.360
THE FUTURE OF GOOGLE:
In the end, Google was iconic for setting

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.940
outrageous goals and going all in regardless
of how ridiculous it makes them look or the

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.690
odds of success.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:06.310
But eversince Larry and Sergey left, Google
has become much more tame mostly due to complacent

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:12.100
employees, a priority to please shareholders,
and a rapidly deteriorating public image.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.630
Given that Google’s revenue and net income
was growing faster than they can count for

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.680
the past several years, Google didn’t have
any problems keeping some out there projects

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:20.680
alive.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:25.190
But, now that Google can feel the pressure
on their bottom line, Google is shifting to

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.130
take decisive action.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:31.149
This isn’t to say that Google will be screwed
or that this move will backfire.

00:12:31.149 --> 00:12:35.000
In fact, I’m sure that this move will work
out great financially.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:40.860
But, at the same time, this is just another
example of Google taking another step closer

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:46.310
to becoming a Cisco or an Intel, and that’s
truly quite a shame.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:47.860
Are you a fan of the New Google?

